WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:05.469
A critical thing we need to keep in mind when building network games is detecting the environment

2
00:00:05.469 --> 00:00:10.630
that we're running in. For that we have the net modes enum. Frankly, the net mode is the

3
00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:14.589
most watertight way of querying whether or not the current environment is a client

4
00:00:14.589 --> 00:00:19.899
or a server one, a fact that often impacts our implementations. Let's take a look at

5
00:00:19.899 --> 00:00:25.109
what net modes come with Unreal and where we might use them. First up we have the

6
00:00:25.109 --> 00:00:30.109
standalone net mode. This executable is running in total isolation and is in no way

7
00:00:30.149 --> 00:00:33.990
subject to a server's authority. We can pretty much surmise that we're running a

8
00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:39.200
single-player game at this point. The dedicated server net mode however is exactly as you'd

9
00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:43.320
expect. It's a dedicated server with no local controllers, only connecting

10
00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:48.969
clients. The listen server net mode is a bit of a blend. This executable is acting

11
00:00:48.969 --> 00:00:53.149
as both client and a server. Other controllers are connecting to this

12
00:00:53.149 --> 00:00:58.899
client with this local client being the host. The client net mode is the

13
00:00:58.899 --> 00:01:02.780
opposite of the dedicated server as you'd expect. This executable is connecting to a

14
00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:07.930
remote authoritative server. Net mode checks often come into play when building

15
00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:11.530
out features that have both front and back ends. So let's take a health

16
00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:14.849
component for instance. We'd want to check that we're currently under the

17
00:01:14.849 --> 00:01:18.730
dedicated server net mode when actually applying damage, but we'd want to check

18
00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:21.810
if we're under the client net mode when playing any on-screen effects or

19
00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:26.290
updating any UI elements. A net mode check is the safest way of checking

20
00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:30.950
you're currently under the appropriate environment. Although thematically

21
00:01:30.989 --> 00:01:35.590
similar to net mode, net role deals more specifically with the authority of the

22
00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:39.750
given circumstance. It won't directly tell us if we're on a server or not but

23
00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:43.349
it will let us know if the current environment is authoritative or an actor.

24
00:01:43.349 --> 00:01:49.560
When we call get local role on an actor we return one of three roles.

25
00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:54.430
Authority lets us know that this actor was spawned locally, this environment

26
00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:58.430
spawned it and can refer to both clients and servers. Should we change

27
00:01:58.469 --> 00:02:02.430
anything about this actor under the authority role we know that A, a server

28
00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:05.950
isn't going to correct it back to another value and B, if we're currently

29
00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:09.750
on a server and this actor is replicated the change may be propagated

30
00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:14.240
down through replication. It really is critical to note that authority

31
00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:18.560
absolutely does not mean server. It expressly means that the actor in

32
00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:22.400
question was spawned locally and is seen as the single source of truth in

33
00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:26.599
regards to how this actor is altered. Case in point, a client responding to

34
00:02:26.599 --> 00:02:30.439
damage locally by spawning VFX would be spawning authoritative particle

35
00:02:30.439 --> 00:02:36.189
systems. Simulated proxy however means the actor was spawned on the server

36
00:02:36.189 --> 00:02:40.430
and replicated down to the local client. It's owned by another client

37
00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:43.830
but requires representation locally. A perfect example of this would be

38
00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:48.210
another player in your world. Some other client owns it but the server

39
00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:52.080
is still authoritative. It has been sent to the local machine because it

40
00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.870
requires some kind of representation. Conversely autonomous proxy is the

41
00:02:56.870 --> 00:03:00.110
other side of the coin. The actor was spawned on the server but the

42
00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:03.750
local machine owns it so that would be your player character in this context.
